#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "180b8d8a-e5ba-53e7-85fe-0018dfc11e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-\\frac{4}{{e}^{4}}$ and ${f}_{max}=4{e}^{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446408} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8beee5-d16b-5ad8-81c1-9b0ed36361cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446414} {:answer/id #uuid "4d624490-8037-521f-bbf8-506c7e05ca1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2{e}^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446420} {:answer/id #uuid "ece1eee0-81d5-54f5-aa04-0c45d7d5fd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-\\frac{1}{e}$ and ${f}_{max}=4{e}^{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446460} {:answer/id #uuid "efa9e65b-368c-592e-ae82-4a598d72fc36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}={e}^{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ece1eee0-81d5-54f5-aa04-0c45d7d5fd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-\\frac{1}{e}$ and ${f}_{max}=4{e}^{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446460} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bc7cdb8-c00a-5bcd-bca0-3c9f09586b74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bdc5f30-6e9d-55a3-aacc-08dbc13064bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-167161" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{x}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region  $D=\\{(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16\\}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(-1,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-4,0)$, and $(4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/dc/dc5a440d527fcf7583c4a341f08f2c6b5f6b4c1dc87b75286ea0a7f569d6babe.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(-1,0)$ lies inside $D,$ we include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-4,0),\\,(4,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4,0) & =-4{e}^{-4}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{e}^{4}} \\\\ f(4,0) & =4{e}^{4}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4{e}^{4} \\\\ f(-1,0) & =-{e}^{-1}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-\\frac{1}{e}$ at the point $(-1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $4{e}^{4}$ at the point $(4,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is a Smooth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cd4c18b-e511-5b14-9f13-a5b4cb4f47f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and  ${f}_{max}=15$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401581} {:answer/id #uuid "2821ee27-e28a-5af2-b0bc-7da60bb40070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401582} {:answer/id #uuid "77dac977-36a1-5c53-9361-88fca32b4abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401583} {:answer/id #uuid "d746e3e5-aa5e-5fa1-88ee-69128529ad71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and  ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401584} {:answer/id #uuid "18156a3d-068d-5e69-8ef7-8771427933c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2821ee27-e28a-5af2-b0bc-7da60bb40070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401582} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc59060d-a9ed-5533-b31b-311227554f8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e7d00266-1ded-5f4c-819a-7467528496c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-167165" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2x,\\,2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x &=0 \\\\ 2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 32\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(4,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(-4,0),\\,(0,-4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(-4,0)$ | $(0,-4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-15$ | $17$ | $-15$ | $17$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-15$ at the points $(4,0)$ and $(-4,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the points $(0,4)$ and $(0,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c23c898-f1a2-5f4e-b367-26b24d6dcd88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446413} {:answer/id #uuid "9e618bea-0b35-5168-96bf-90ae9dca8ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446441} {:answer/id #uuid "c070f661-5b43-5330-b698-b38f14c4686a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446450} {:answer/id #uuid "c137dca3-ade7-592f-92eb-70a9b9c0e301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446451} {:answer/id #uuid "d23eb270-d0e1-5d1c-a620-fc71aa88f9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c23c898-f1a2-5f4e-b367-26b24d6dcd88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446413} :answer-field/id #uuid "da2953ed-d903-58c0-9224-6075f8a7a3be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8052460c-148b-5893-b35a-1e1c771c0935" :question/math-academy-id "q-83423" :question/problem "Find the global minimum of the function $f(x,y)=xy-x-y$ on the closed square $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,1)$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/7f/7fe1aa21de9b6354e561eb1e82f6c83b25c71770470dc8501e6b491e35e2c391.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,1)$ is in the interior of $D$, we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(2,2),\\,(0,2).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =(1)(1)-1-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(0,0) & =(0)(0)-0-0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)(0)-2-0 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(2,2) & =(2)(2)-2-2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,2) & =(0)(2)-0-2 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(2,0)$and $(0,2),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the points $(0,0)$ and $(2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is a Smooth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46bc837a-bbcd-5027-bfb5-1c8984cd1027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446416} {:answer/id #uuid "601699b9-c391-5b8e-a88a-c98ed2ca1d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=18$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446423} {:answer/id #uuid "9f15347b-2581-5183-b776-03898fbfc72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446442} {:answer/id #uuid "a6701104-c6a3-5c18-9c13-31528c875706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446446} {:answer/id #uuid "cda725f3-ec0d-574a-ba5c-8fd6946ee6a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "601699b9-c391-5b8e-a88a-c98ed2ca1d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=18$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446423} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c7409f8-33ba-5d76-9294-d18b4e765a0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "004a7cc4-06c2-5abf-9f5d-0dfb86998289" :question/math-academy-id "q-83434" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x+2$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(1,0)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(1,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =2(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(1)+2 \\\\  & =2+0-4+2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(-2,0) & =2(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-2)+2 \\\\  & =8+0+8+2 \\\\  & =18 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)+2 \\\\  & =8+0-8+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $18$ at the point $(-2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is a Smooth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70ee89eb-54ab-5070-99c6-c324c0f9f01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446425} {:answer/id #uuid "a52483f7-a6b6-5cc1-abb3-7db0b579ef56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446444} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7ee3bb-67c1-5270-8d84-3e8e37bf67a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446447} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a76143-6f93-57f5-9713-8433062ded47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446448} {:answer/id #uuid "efcb8428-944f-5ad5-960c-f08b95cb6ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70ee89eb-54ab-5070-99c6-c324c0f9f01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446425} :answer-field/id #uuid "81180c93-e1c2-5e9f-adf8-f0c58db4c467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2f4f1da-3ab7-5268-ad0e-cfd1ed45e614" :question/math-academy-id "q-83437" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.$ You are given that the function has no critical points." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nSince f has no critical points in D , the list of candidates for the extrema won't contain critical points.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\le 1$, this equation gives the solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval,  $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain candidate points: \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},0),\\,(0,1),\\,(0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(0,-1) & ={0}^{2}+(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(0,1).$"}]}
